    {q:"Coal is a fossil fuel that is also a sedimentary rock, but petroleum (oil) is not a sedimentary rock. Explain the difference between them.",memo:"Coal is a solid rock that forms from ancient plant material. Petroleum (oil) is a liquid fossil fuel, so it is not a rock.",r:{"kw":["liquid|fluid|runny|not solid|not a solid|isnt solid"]}},
    {q:"Name TWO things you could look for when describing a sedimentary rock sample.",memo:"Any two: colour, grain size, layers, visible fragments, fossils.",r:{"kw":["colour|color","grain|grains|texture|rough|smooth","layer|layers|stripe|band","fragment|piece|bits","fossil"],"min":2}},
    {q:"A learner identifies a rock only by its colour. Explain why this may lead to a mistake.",memo:"Different rocks can look the same colour, so colour alone is not enough. You also need to observe grain size, layers, fragments and fossils.",r:{"kw":["similar|alike|look the same|looks the same|same colour|same color|vary|varies|different rocks|many rocks|other feature|other thing|more than colour|more than color|grain|layer|fossil|texture|hardness|other observ|other clue|more observ|more feature|other way|more information"]}},
    {q:"A rock sample is made of very fine particles and splits into thin layers. Which sedimentary rock might it be? Give a reason, and explain why your answer is a possibility and not a certainty.",memo:"Possibly shale. Very fine particles that split into thin layers fit shale, but other rocks can have similar features, so more observations are needed before being sure.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["shale"]},{"kw":["possib|might|maybe|could|may|not sure|not certain|not definite|other rock|more|check|need|test|unsure|cant be sure|cannot be sure|similar"]}]}},
    {q:"A learner says: “Every sedimentary rock contains fossils.” Explain why this statement is a problem.",memo:"Words like ‘every’ leave no room for exceptions. Not every sedimentary rock contains fossils, so scientists look at more than one feature.",r:{"kw":["exception|not every|not all|some|sometimes|doesnt have|dont have|do not have|doesnt always|isnt always|no layers|without layers|too strong|absolute|no room|may not|might not|not always|can have no|not have"]}},
    {q:"In a bottle model, a mix of gravel, sand and mud in water is shaken, then left still. What does the model show about deposition?",memo:"The particles settle at different levels: the biggest and heaviest settle first, forming layers when the water is still.",r:{"kw":["layer","level","different|sizes|size|heavier|bigger|larger|smaller|lighter","settle|sink|fall|bottom|drop"],"min":2}},
    {q:"Explain why a sand-and-salt model cannot show that real sedimentary rock forms in a short time.",memo:"The model only shows part of the process (how a dissolved material can bind grains). Real sedimentary rock needs compaction and cementation over a very long time.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["long|years|time|slow|centur|millions|thousand"]},{"kw":["compact|cement|press|only|just|part|step|stage|not the whole|missing|doesn|does not|isn"]}]}},
    {q:"What is a fossil? Name TWO things that can become fossils.",memo:"A fossil is the preserved remains, impression or trace of an organism that lived long ago. Any two: shell, bone, leaf, footprint.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["preserved|remains|trace|imprint|impression|left behind|long ago|ancient|millions|died|dead|old"]},{"kw":["shell","bone","leaf|leaves","footprint|footprints|tracks","tooth|teeth","plant","skeleton|dinosaur|animal"],"min":2}]}},
    {q:"In a cliff of undisturbed layers, which layer is generally the youngest, and why?",memo:"The top layer. It was deposited last, on top of all the older layers below it.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["top|upper|highest|above"]},{"kw":["last|latest|after|newest|most recent|later|on top of|came last|deposited last"]}]}},
    {q:"In an undisturbed cliff, Layer X (bottom) contains fish bones and Layer Z (top) contains leaf fossils. What might this suggest? Use careful words such as may, might or suggests.",memo:"The area may have changed over time: the fish bones may suggest water covered the area when Layer X formed, and the leaf fossils may suggest land with plants later, when Layer Z formed. (Accept careful, evidence-based answers; do not accept ‘it proves’.)",r:{"all":[{"kw":["may|might|suggest|could|possibly|perhaps|probably|seem|likely"]},{"kw":["change|changed|different|environment|habitat|conditions|over time|later|before|once|used to|lived"]},{"kw":["sea|water|ocean|marine|lake|wet|river|land|grew|grow|tree|forest|dry|plants"]}]}},
    {q:"Use claim–evidence–reasoning. Claim: “Layer X (at the bottom) is older than Layer Z (at the top).” Write the evidence and the reasoning.",memo:"Evidence: Layer X lies underneath Layer Z. Reasoning: in an undisturbed sequence, lower layers were deposited before the layers above them.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["under|underneath|below|bottom|beneath|lies|lower|lowest"]},{"kw":["first|before|earlier|deposited|laid|settled|came|undisturbed|below|beneath|underneath"]}]}},
    {q:"A scientist finds one leaf fossil. Can she tell exactly what the whole area looked like from this fossil? Explain, and name TWO other pieces of evidence that would help.",memo:"No – a fossil alone does not tell the whole story. Any two helpful extras: rock type, grain size, layer position/thickness, other fossils, whether the layers have been disturbed.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["no$|not|cannot|cant|isn|alone|doesn|wont|unable|impossible"]},{"kw":["rock","grain","layer","position|thickness","other fossil|more fossil|fossils","disturb|folded|tilted|broken","colour|color"],"min":2}]}},
    {q:"Explain why scientists look at several different clues before they decide what an area was like long ago.",memo:"One clue can be misleading or incomplete. Comparing rock type, layer position, fossils and other evidence helps to check an idea before drawing a conclusion.",r:{"kw":["wrong|mislead|incomplete|not enough|mistake|whole story|check|compare|confirm|to be sure|be sure|make sure|accurate|reliable|more evidence|proof|prove|stronger|better|full picture|complete|checking"]}},
