    {q:"What is a mineral? What is a mine?",memo:"A mineral is a naturally occurring substance found in the Earth’s crust. A mine is a place where minerals or other valuable materials are extracted from the ground.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["naturally occurring|natural|nature|found in|earth|ground|crust|substance|rock|material"]},{"kw":["place|where|dig|extract|removed|taken|dug|hole|site|area|get"]}]}},
    {q:"Match each resource to a common use: platinum, gold, coal, iron ore.  Uses: A electricity generation  B making steel  C catalytic converters in cars  D jewellery and electronics.",memo:"platinum – C; gold – D; coal – A; iron ore – B. All four must be correct.",r:{"pairs":[["platinum","c|catalytic|converter|car"],["gold","d|jewel|electronic"],["coal","a|electricity|generat|power"],["iron","b|steel"]]}},
    {q:"Name ONE use of copper and ONE use of diamonds.",memo:"Copper: electrical wiring, electronics or plumbing. Diamonds: jewellery or industrial cutting tools.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["wir|electric|cable|plumb|pipe|electronic"]},{"kw":["jewel|ring|cut|cutting|tool|drill|industrial"]}]}},
    {q:"Explain why iron ore is a useful resource for making buildings, tools and machinery.",memo:"Iron ore is used to make iron and steel, which are strong and hard, so they are good for buildings, tools and machinery. (A resource’s use depends on its properties.)",r:{"kw":["strong|hard|tough|durable|sturdy|strength|last long|steel"]}},
    {q:"You are given a map of South Africa with coloured dots for different minerals. What should you check before you decide where gold is found, and why?",memo:"Check the key (legend) first. The key tells you what each colour or symbol stands for, so you know which dots show gold.",r:{"kw":["key|legend","mean|tell|represent|stand|show|see which|which colour|which symbol|what each|what the"]}},
    {q:"A learner says: “Gold is found everywhere in South Africa.” Explain why this should not be assumed, and how a map would show it.",memo:"Minerals are only found in certain places and each province has different resources. A map’s symbols and colours show where each mineral is found, so you must check the map and key.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["different|vary|varies|some provinces|only some|certain|concentrat|uneven|not all|not every|isnt|arent|doesnt|differ|each province|only in|only found|specific"]},{"kw":["symbol|colour|colours|key|legend|dots|pattern|areas|places|where"]}]}},
    {q:"Explain why a town may start to grow near a new mine.",memo:"The mine creates jobs, so workers move there and need homes, shops, schools and roads. This can make a town grow near the mine.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["job|work|employ|workers|miners|people"]},{"kw":["house|home|live|shop|school|road|service|town|hospital|clinic|accommodation|stay"]}]}},
    {q:"Give TWO things, other than minerals, that a mining company needs before a new mining town can succeed.",memo:"Any two: transport (roads or rail), water, investment (money), good working conditions, and the mining staying profitable.",r:{"kw":["transport|road|rail|train|port|access","water","invest|money|funding|capital","working condition|safety|wages|pay|conditions|workers","viable|profit|price|economic|worth|afford|cost|demand|run out|depleted|enough mineral|still"],"min":2}},
    {q:"What is open-cast (surface) mining? When is it a suitable method?",memo:"The top layers of soil and rock are removed, often creating a large open pit. It is suitable when the deposit is close enough to the surface to be reached economically from above.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["top|surface|above|open|pit|remove|removing|dig|digging|stripped|layers"]},{"kw":["close|near|shallow|just under|not deep|reach|easy|cheap|top of"]}]}},
    {q:"What is underground mining? When is it used?",memo:"Shafts and tunnels give access to the deposit, and workers and machines extract the mineral underground. It is used when the deposit is deep below the surface.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["shaft|tunnel"]},{"kw":["deep|far down|below the surface|deeper|too far|underground deposit"]}]}},
    {q:"Explain TWO differences between surface mining and underground mining.",memo:"Surface mining removes the top layers to make an open pit and changes the land a lot; underground mining uses shafts and tunnels, so the surface changes less (but it is more dangerous and costly).",r:{"all":[{"kw":["open|pit|excavation|top|above|surface"]},{"kw":["shaft|tunnel"]},{"kw":["visible|obvious|damage|change|big|bigger|large|extensive|less|more|hole|scar|huge"]}]}},
    {q:"A mineral deposit lies only a few metres below the surface. Which mining method is more suitable, and why?",memo:"Surface (open-cast) mining. The deposit is close to the surface and can be reached by removing the top layers.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["surface|open cast|open pit|opencast|open"]},{"kw":["close|near|shallow|reach|easy|cheap|cheaper|simple|quick|not deep|remove|dig|economic"]}]}},
    {q:"Give TWO dangers that miners may face working deep underground.",memo:"Any two: rock falls or collapses, heat, poor ventilation (little fresh air, dust or gas), flooding, ground subsidence.",r:{"kw":["rock fall|rockfall|falling rock|collapse|cave","heat|hot|temperature","ventilation|air|breath|suffocat|dust|gas|oxygen|smoke","subsidence|sink|sinkhole|flood|explos|trapped|accident"],"min":2}},
    {q:"Complete the cause-and-effect chain: Mining removes vegetation → ______ → ______ → soil is washed into a river.",memo:"soil becomes exposed → rain washes the soil away. (Both gaps needed, in this order.)",r:{"order":["expos|bare|uncover|loose|nothing hold|no roots|no plants","rain|wash|carr|water|flow|wind|erosion|erode"]}},
    {q:"SOURCE E: A photograph shows a quarry where rock is blasted and taken away by trucks. The hillside beside the quarry has no trees, and dust covers the leaves of nearby plants.  (a) Which mining method is shown?  (b) Describe TWO visible effects on the environment.",memo:"(a) Surface/open-cast mining (a quarry). (b) Any two: trees have been removed; dust covers the plants; the land is blasted and reshaped; noise from blasting and trucks.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["surface|open cast|open pit|opencast|open|quarry"]},{"kw":["trees|vegetation|plants|cleared|removed|bare","dust|air|pollut|dirty|covered|leaves","blast|noise|rock|hole|pit|trucks"],"min":2}]}},
    {q:"Using Source E (a hillside with no trees beside a mine), explain how heavy rain could affect a river downstream.",memo:"Rain can wash the bare soil and dust from the hillside into the river, making the water muddy with sediment. This can harm fish and other living things.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["wash|carry|carried|flow|run|erod|move|sweep|flood"]},{"kw":["river|stream|water"]},{"kw":["mud|muddy|sediment|dirty|cloudy|brown|pollut|silt|clog|harm|kill|fish|animals|plants|habitat|organism|flood"]}]}},
    {q:"Mining can harm the environment in several ways. Name THREE, and explain how ONE of them could affect people or animals.",memo:"Any three of: land disturbance, water pollution, air pollution, habitat destruction, noise pollution, waste. One explained, e.g. polluted water can make people and animals sick.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["land|disturb|landscape|soil","water|river|pollut","air|dust|emission|smog","habitat|animals|destroy|plants|biodiversity","noise|loud|blast","waste"],"min":3},{"kw":["because|so |which|can |could|causes|leads|harm|kill|damage|contaminat|affect|result|means"]}]}},
    {q:"Chemicals from a mine leak into groundwater. Explain the cause-and-effect chain that could affect people.",memo:"Chemicals leak into the groundwater → the water becomes polluted → people who drink it, or use it for crops, can become sick.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["pollut|dirty|contaminat|poison|toxic|chemical|unsafe"]},{"kw":["sick|ill$|harm|hurt|die|disease|drink|health|unsafe|crops|animals|damage"]}]}},
    {q:"What does it mean to rehabilitate a mine site? Give TWO actions.",memo:"To rehabilitate means to repair or restore land affected by mining. Any two: replace suitable soil and reshape the land; replant indigenous vegetation; control erosion; treat or contain polluted water; secure dangerous open pits; monitor the site after mining.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["repair|restor|fix|rebuild|heal|back to|return|reclaim|recover|clean"]},{"kw":["soil","plant|replant|tree|grass|vegetation|seed","reshap|level|slope|fill","water|treat|clean|pollut","erosion|erod","pit|secure|fence|close|cover","monitor|check|watch"],"min":2}]}},
    {q:"A mine has closed and left open pits and bare, eroding slopes. Explain why this is a problem, then recommend ONE action and justify it.",memo:"The bare slopes will keep eroding and pollute rivers, and the open pits are dangerous for people and animals. A good recommendation (e.g. replant vegetation and reshape the slopes, and fence or fill the pits) must explain how the action reduces the harm.",r:{"all":[{"kw":["contaminat|poison|dirty|harm|erod|unsafe|danger|destroy|ruin|unhealthy|hurt|kill|spread|run off|rivers|groundwater|animals|people|plants"]},{"kw":["clean|replant|plant|repair|reshape|rehabilit|fix|fill|cover|purif|rebuild|soil|vegetation|trees|grass|filter|safe"]},{"kw":["because|so |which|will|help|reduce|stop|prevent|protect|means|reason|otherwise|would"]}]}},
